<h2>Two defences, both late</h2>
<p>The accused, a tenant of the complainant since 2018, ran two inconsistent explanations for the Rs 7 lakh received by bank transfer: that the first tranche paid for sanitary goods, and that the cheques were mere security under a lease agreement. The High Court&rsquo;s judgment is a study in how each failed for want of foundation rather than want of imagination.</p>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The goods plea.</strong> Over Rs 7 lakh of goods allegedly supplied &mdash; yet not one bill, GST record or transport receipt on the file. Silence in the notice reply and at the Section 251 stage marked the plea as an afterthought.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The lease plea.</strong> An unstamped photocopy of a lease deed, original never produced, no notice to produce served, and the complainant never confronted with it in cross-examination &mdash; the document simply never entered the evidentiary field.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Financial capacity.</strong> The attack on the complainant&rsquo;s means collapsed against the fact that both advances moved through banking channels.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Concurrent findings.</strong> With two courts having appraised the same record, revision offered no third bite: no perversity or jurisdictional error was shown.</div>
</div>

<h2>The discipline of secondary evidence</h2>
<p>The judgment turns substantially on a procedural rule that decides more cheque trials than any presumption: a party relying on a document must prove it. A photocopy is secondary evidence, admissible only through the gateway conditions of the Evidence Act &mdash; which required, at minimum, a notice to the opposite party to produce the original. None was given. Nor was the document put to the complainant in cross-examination, so the defence narrative was never tested against the person best placed to deny it. By the time the plea matured in revision, it was argument without evidence.</p>

<h2>Timing is the other half of credibility</h2>
<p>Delhi courts consistently treat the reply to the statutory demand notice, and the accused&rsquo;s plea when notice is framed under Section 251 CrPC (now Section 274 BNSS for post-July 2024 trials), as the natural moments for a genuine defence to surface. A defence appearing for the first time at trial &mdash; or in appeal &mdash; starts with a credibility deficit that documentary corroboration alone can repair. Here there was neither timing nor corroboration.</p>

<h2>The endgame</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Sentence and compensation.</strong> Six months&rsquo; simple imprisonment with a Rs 10 lakh fine (one month in default) stood affirmed; the petitioner was directed to appear before the trial court on 20 November 2026 for payment and execution of the sentence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For drafting practice.</strong> Reply to every Section 138 notice on the record the client can prove; the reply is the first exhibit of the defence.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>For revisions.</strong> Revisional success against concurrent cheque convictions requires a defect of law &mdash; an unproved document and an unpleaded story will not reopen facts.</div>
</div>
